   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5): APPROVE** — head 5e8aaffee927a5f478f539c9ed73c7e067aba089\n\nRound 1 findings:\n1. Fixed. `App/README.md:25`: the documented command now passes `-parallel-testing-enabled NO`, matching `.github/workflows/ci.yml:206`. A new paragraph explains why.\n2. Fixed. `.github/workflows/ci.yml:199-200`: the comment names #717 and the scheme's `parallelizable = \"YES\"`. I checked both claims. The setting is at `App/SwiftHFR.xcodeproj/xcshareddata/xcschemes/SwiftHFR.xcscheme:35`, and `git log -S parallelizable` traces it to 9d4afb4 (#717).\n3. Accepted as before. There is still no macOS run, so the timing claim waits for the first ready run.\n\nNew findings:\n1. Detail, `.github/workflows/ci.yml:199`: this comment line is 205 characters. The neighbouring comment lines are at most about 123. No lint enforces a limit, so nothing fails. Fix (optional): wrap it after \"(added by #717).\"\n\nNo regression: the change is two files, config and docs only. No test, no Swift source and no scheme file is touched. The ci.yml `run` command still parses, and the new flag sits inside the folded `>-` scalar on the `-destination` line. No other `xcodebuild … test` command for the SwiftHFR scheme exists in the repo.\n\n(Verdict relayed verbatim from the review sub-agent, round 2 of 2. The optional line-wrap is left as is, to avoid another CI-tested push.)\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
